  --debug               log each API request and retry (secrets redacted)
  --no-color            disable colors (also honours NO_COLOR)

Commands:
  list [options]        List webhook endpoints
  create [options]      Register an https endpoint (prints the signing secret
                        once)
  delete <id>           Delete a webhook endpoint (deliveries stop immediately)
  test [options] <url>  Send a correctly signed sample event to your receiver
                        (no API key needed)
"
`;

exports[`--help > invoice-ai webhooks test --help 1`] = `
"Usage: invoice-ai webhooks test [options] <url>

Send a correctly signed sample event to your receiver (no API key needed)

Options:
  --secret <whsec>      the endpoint's signing secret (whsec_…)
  --event <type>        event type to send (default: "invoice.paid")
  -h, --help            show help

Global Options:
  -v, --version         print the CLI version
  --api-key <key>       API key to use (overrides INVOICE_AI_API_KEY and the
                        saved profile)
  -p, --profile <name>  saved profile to use (see \`invoice-ai switch\`)
  --json                print raw JSON (default when output is piped)
  --format <format>     output format (choices: "table", "json", "csv")
  -y, --yes             skip confirmation prompts
  --debug               log each API request and retry (secrets redacted)
  --no-color            disable colors (also honours NO_COLOR)

Signs the body exactly like a real delivery (Standard Webhooks: webhook-id,
webhook-timestamp, webhook-signature), so your verification code runs for real.

Example:
  $ invoice-ai webhooks test http://localhost:3000/webhooks --secret whsec_…
"
`;

